\f1\fs22 Chanta regained consciousness slowly, his head throbbing in time with the gentle rumble of an engine. He opened his eyes and found himself in the back seat of what appeared to be a meticulously restored 1965 Cadillac Fleetwood, the kind of car that existed only in museums and private collections of the obscenely wealthy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, the Great Teacher awakens,\'94 said a familiar voice beside him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta turned, blinking away the fog in his vision, and saw the High Elvis sitting next to him in full regalia\'97white jumpsuit, cape, sunglasses, and that unmistakable confident smile. The car was moving through familiar streets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Did you just kidnap me?\'94 Chanta asked, his voice rough.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Recruited,\'94 the High Elvis corrected. \'93With, admittedly, some aggressive persuasion tactics.\'94 He gestured out the window. \'93Look at your kingdom, Great Teacher. See what\'92s become of it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi Quarter had changed in ways Chanta hadn\'92t anticipated. Where once the streets had been unified in their reverence for all things Elvis, now they were sharply divided. On one side, traditional Elvi establishments still operated as before, though with increased security and suspicious glances toward anyone approaching from the opposite side of the street. Across from them, new storefronts had emerged, featuring crosses alongside Elvis iconography and signs promising \'93Salvation Through Song.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What the tax happened here?\'94 Chanta muttered as the Cadillac glided through the transformed district.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Schism,\'94 the High Elvis replied. The smile didn\'92t leave his face, but the rest of him had gone hard. \'93The government seeded a weaponised gospel contagion.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta looked at him. \'93Contagion.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They swapped jukebox records,\'94 the High Elvis said. \'93Gospel everywhere. You can\'92t drink, dance, or pick a fight without getting a hymn wedged into your skull.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They did what?\'94 Chanta\'92s head snapped up, immediately regretting the sudden movement as pain shot through his skull. \'93And nobody thought to tell me?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ve been... unavailable,\'94 the High Elvis said diplomatically. \'93Living in several laps of luxury, I believe you termed it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The car turned down a familiar street, passing what had been the Memphis Mafia Bar & Grill. The famous neon sign now sported a hastily added \'93& House of Worship\'94 beneath the original name. Armed Elvi guards stood at the entrance, checking patrons for prohibited items.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Pull over,\'94 Chanta said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis didn\'92t argue. The Cadillac eased into the kerb with the kind of silent obedience that came from money.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta shoved the door open and stepped out into the street air, blinking against it. The bar looked like someone had taken a beloved local institution and stapled a church to its forehead. A queue of Elvi in modest jumpsuits and no-sequin austerity waited to enter, heads bowed, mouths moving as if practising restraint. No one wore a cape.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the door, an Elvis in a plain black jacket stood like a moral bouncer. He patted down a man with the gentle creepiness of a priest copping a feel as much as looking for contraband joy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta strode toward him anyway, the way you walked into a place you used to belong.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bouncer\'92s eyes flicked over Chanta\'97up, down, and then to the face, lingering a fraction too long. Recognition without warmth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No,\'94 the bouncer said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta blinked. \'93No what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No entry.\'94 The Elvis\'92s voice was calm, rehearsed, and therefore more threatening than shouting. \'93This place is for the saved.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m\'97\'94 Chanta started, then stopped himself. He wasn\'92t sure what he was anymore, but he knew what he wasn\'92t: saved.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind the bouncer, inside the bar, Chanta glimpsed movement\'97people packed shoulder to shoulder, a stage light pulsing like a heartbeat, and a line of kneeling heads.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis came up beside him, cape swaying as if it had its own agenda. He didn\'92t speak. He didn\'92t have to. The message was: look at it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta swallowed. He was close enough now to hear it\'97soft singing, not rock and roll, not the joyous brawling harmonies of his own crowd, but something flat and pious that made the hairs on his arms lift.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A muffled cheer rose, then fell into a hush.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta leaned a fraction to the side, trying to see past the bouncer\'92s shoulder.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bouncer shifted too, blocking him without appearing to move.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Right,\'94 Chanta muttered. \'93Got it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He turned away like a man accepting a decision, walked three steps, then stopped by a shopfront window that reflected the bar\'92s entrance. He reached into his pocket, found the sunglasses he\'92d been wearing when he woke up in a stranger\'92s car, and put them on. They were ludicrously dark and too large for his face. They made him look like an Asian Elvis impersonator who\'92d been through hard times and had no interest in discussing them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta glanced at the High Elvis. \'93I\'92m going in.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92re not,\'94 the High Elvis said mildly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta nodded as if he\'92d been chastised by a teacher and had taken it to heart. Then he walked back toward the queue.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He slid in behind a pair of robe-wearing Elvi, lowered his chin, and folded his posture into the shape of penitence. He kept his hands visible and his mouth shut. If there was anything he\'92d learned from being dragged out of bed by zealots, it was that zealots respected compliance almost as much as violence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The line shuffled forward.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bouncer\'92s hands patted down the first robe. No weapons, no bottles, no glitter. Approved.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The second robe stepped up. Approved.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stepped forward.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bouncer\'92s hands stopped at Chanta\'92s ribs. Not because he\'92d found anything. Because the bouncer had simply decided to stop.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You,\'94 the bouncer said quietly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta did not look up. He breathed as if he had been taught to breathe by someone who disapproved of breathing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You smell like bars,\'94 the bouncer said. \'93Not worship.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind the bouncer, someone inside began to speak, voice amplified, the cadence unmistakable even through the wall of bodies: preacher rhythm, showman timing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta kept his head bowed. He took one step sideways, as if being pushed by the crowd.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bouncer\'92s hand tightened on his shoulder.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta let his weight go slack in his knees, like a man overcome by the Spirit. He sagged forward with just enough theatricality to be annoying, but not enough to be suspicious. His sunglasses slid crooked. His mouth made a soft sound that could have been a sob, or indigestion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi behind him tutted sympathetically, then stepped in to help, hands on Chanta\'92s arms, guiding him\'97of course they guided him\'97through the doorway.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Saved,\'94 one murmured, with the delighted superiority of someone who\'92d found a new way to be better than you.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bouncer hesitated. If he dragged Chanta back out, he\'92d have to argue with believers, and believers were exhausting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So he let them carry Chanta in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, the Memphis Mafia Bar had been rearranged into something that wanted to be a church and couldn\'92t quite forget it was a bar. The booths were still there, but people sat facing the stage instead of each other, as if conversation was a sin. Rhinestones had been prised off walls and replaced with cheap wooden crosses. A pile of confiscated items sat on a table near the door\'97hip flasks, sequinned belts, a pair of glitter boots, and a small jar of hair that made Chanta\'92s stomach turn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There were no pool tables. Chanta also suspected there were no smashed toilet bowls in the bathrooms. Even worse, he couldn\'92t see evidence of a bullet hole anywhere.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On stage, a line of Elvi knelt. Another line stood behind them holding scissors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s robe-handlers pushed him down onto the floor at the back, near the wall, like someone placing an offering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The preacher\'92s voice rose over the room, tinny through cheap speakers and thick with conviction.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'97PRIDE,\'94 he thundered, and the crowd shuddered as if pride was a physical contaminant. \'93Pride is the sin that cast Lucifer from heaven!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta leaned forward just enough to see.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the front of the stage, half-glimpsed behind a pulpit cobbled out of something that looked suspiciously like an old bar lectern, stood an Elvis in a plain suit with the face of a man who\'92d once played a doctor and now played God. His hair was immaculate. His eyes were burning. His smile was the sort you bought with money you didn\'92t earn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Pastor Rex Humble.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta felt the room tilt. Not with dizziness. With wrongness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>One of the kneeling Elvi stood and approached the pulpit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Humble lifted the scissors with ceremony while the crowd sang a hymn with pelvis-free solemnity.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta snorted with derision. 
\f2\i The title was too obvious
\f1\i0 , he thought. 
\f2\i It should have been called 
\f1\i0 Amazing Grass
\f2\i .\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How sweet the smoke,\'94 Chanta hummed to himself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Humble snipped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A sideburn fell.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The kneeling Elvis flinched as if he\'92d been hit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Humble dropped the hair into a crystal decanter on the pulpit, already half-filled with dark curls like some obscene relic collection. The crowd murmured approval.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s hands curled into fists in his lap. He couldn\'92t tell if he wanted to laugh, vomit, or start a fight he couldn\'92t win.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis appeared beside him as if he\'92d been there all along, cape somehow muted among the beige robes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta hissed, barely moving his lips. \'93This is\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Contagion,\'94 the High Elvis murmured back. \'93It spreads by song. By repetition. By being in the room.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On stage, Humble raised his hands and the crowd rose with him, swaying in unison, a sea of obedience.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s throat tightened. He understood, with a clarity that made him hate himself, how easy it would be to get caught in it. How easy it would be to let the rhythm do the thinking.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He forced himself to look away, to break the spell, to find anger and hold on to it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Come on,\'94 the High Elvis said, and his voice had lost its showmanship. \'93You\'92ve seen enough.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta let himself be hauled up by the robe-wearers again, sagging one more time for good measure, and was carried back toward the door with the reverent fuss people reserved for public suffering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Outside, the air felt wrong too\'97cleaner, but not kinder.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stood on the footpath, staring at the sign: Memphis Mafia Bar & Grill & House of Worship. As if you could just bolt a religion onto a building and call it salvation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He swallowed bile and rage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So what\'92s your festival about?\'94 he asked, because if he didn\'92t speak in normal sentences, he was going to start screaming.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Unity through music,\'94 the High Elvis replied. \'93We need to remind our people what we truly believe in. Science, evolution, the power of knowledge over superstition.\'94 He looked directly at Chanta. \'93And we need the voice that wrote 
\f2\i Evolve Me Tender
\f1\i0  to speak for us again.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta stared back toward the door, hearing the hymn through the walls like a parasite.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I haven\'92t written anything new in months,\'94 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then you\'92ll have to dig deep, Great Teacher. Because if we lose this battle for hearts and minds, the Elvi might never recover.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Cadillac eased back into motion. It turned one final corner and revealed a sight that made Chanta\'92s breath catch\'97the massive outdoor concert space being constructed for the festival, already drawing crowds of excited Evolution Elvi. Stage techs tested lighting rigs as massive speakers were wheeled into position.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So,\'94 the High Elvis said, \'93are you with us? Or do we need another pool cue?\'94
